<p>Latvian Prime Minister Evika Siliņa said Thursday she would resign after losing parliamentary support from the left-leaning Progressives, a coalition partner that withdrew its backing after she dismissed Defense Minister Andris Sprūds over incidents involving stray Ukrainian drones. Her resignation brings down the coalition led by her center-right New Unity party months before elections due in October. The dispute followed incidents in which Ukrainian drones aimed at Russia entered Latvian airspace from Russia and crashed in Latvia; several sources said two drones struck or came down at oil or energy facilities. Announcing her resignation, Siliņa accused political rivals of putting party interests ahead of responsibility. President Edgars Rinkēvičs, who must select a new head of government, is due to meet parliamentary parties on Friday; the outgoing government will remain in office until a new Cabinet is approved.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ntDf97Y-BlIawsNEKk6DW5jCMCK1r3qivAoNK-Agj84M3Hk52d1zj_ET1xyLFK1QspND79CPzh-IgQgcNoPlsqfDr_4awliGt3tJ3T3cOg' alt='Latvia&#x27;s Prime Minister Evika Silina.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>No-confidence pressure: BBC Russian reported that the Saeima could have voted Thursday on an opposition demand to dismiss Siliņa, while two of the coalition&#x27;s three parties had blamed her for the government&#x27;s collapse.</li><li>Defense post: After Sprūds left the Defense Ministry, Siliņa nominated Latvian army Col. Raivis Melnis instead of another Progressives representative, deepening the coalition dispute.</li><li>Alert criticism: Critics cited late public alerts, with Taiwan&#x27;s CNA reporting that mobile warnings were issued only after the drones had crashed.</li><li>Damage report: Kurier reported that the drones fell at an oil depot in Rēzekne, where tanks were empty and there were no deaths, injuries or major damage.</li></ul><blockquote>I am stepping down, but I am not giving up - Evika Siliņa</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Evika Siliņa: Siliņa described her resignation as a difficult but honest decision and said her work had prioritized national security and residents&#x27; welfare. (<a href='https://www.cna.com.tw/news/aopl/202605140319.aspx'>Central News Agency</a>)</li><li>Progressives party: Kurier reported that the party had asked Rinkēvičs to open consultations on forming a new government. (<a href='https://kurier.at/politik/ausland/lettland-regierungschefin-tritt-zurueck/403159828'>Kurier</a>)</li><li>Andris Suvajevs: Progressives leader Andris Suvajevs called Siliņa&#x27;s resignation the right decision and said he regretted her decision to blame other coalition partners for the government&#x27;s collapse. (<a href='https://www.cna.com.tw/news/aopl/202605140319.aspx'>Central News Agency</a>)</li><li>Edgars Rinkēvičs: The president&#x27;s office said Rinkēvičs had received Siliņa&#x27;s resignation letter. (<a href='https://kurier.at/politik/ausland/lettland-regierungschefin-tritt-zurueck/403159828'>Kurier</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/latvian-prime-minister-quits-after-backlash-over-stray-ukrainian-drone-strikes/'>Latvian prime minister quits after backlash over stray Ukrainian drone strikes</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/latvia-prime-minister-evika-silina-resigns-over-handling-of-drone-incidents-after-coalition-ally-s-withdrawal/a-77159518'>Latvia: Prime Minister Evika Silina resigns over handling of drone incidents after coalition ally&#x27;s withdrawal</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/may/14/russia-ukraine-strikes-poland-defence-talks-latest-news-updates'>Latvia prime minister resigns days after ‘stray’ drone incursion – Europe live</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/639977-latvian-prime-minister-resigns/'>NATO member’s government collapses after Ukrainian drone incident</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='ht